WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.780
In the "Fast and Furious" series,&nbsp;
death is little more than a speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.380
bump. So it's not a huge shock that a&nbsp;
character returns after their seeming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.740
end in "Fast X," but you do have&nbsp;
to wonder just how it happened.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.680
Obviously, spoilers for "Fast X" ahead.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.120
There's a lot of ridiculous plot twists that&nbsp;
happen throughout director Louis Leterrier and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:23.520
producer/star Vin Diesel's bombastic magnum&nbsp;
opus, "Fast X." But none are quite as hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.420
to swallow as the final scene's shocking&nbsp;
return. No, we're not talking about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.320
long-spoiled mid-credits scene that brings&nbsp;
Dwayne Jonson's Luke Hobbs back into the fold.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:36.060
That sequence is astounding for its own&nbsp;
reasons, but what truly defies logic is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.680
the revelation that, for some unknown period&nbsp;
of time, Gisele Yashar has been hiding in what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.340
appears to be the nuclear submarine from&nbsp;
"The Fate of the Furious." The character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.380
first appeared in 2009's "Fast and Furious"&nbsp;
played by future "Wonder Woman" star Gal Gadot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.460
and met her tragic demise in "Fast&nbsp;
and Furious 6," or so we thought.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.640
Like her former lover Han, Gisele&nbsp;
has returned from the dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.000
bringing with her a whole slew of&nbsp;
unanswered questions. Then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.020
the series had already laid the groundwork&nbsp;
for a tenuously reasonable explanation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.340
her return -- though whether it increases or&nbsp;
undermines the dramatic tension is up for debate.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:16.560
"After Gisele died, I didn't&nbsp;
know what to do. I was aimless."

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.720
In hindsight, it would almost be more surprising&nbsp;
if Gisele hadn't survived her "Fast 6" demise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.940
Characters from this franchise have survived&nbsp;
fiery explosions, gunshots to the head, and about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.340
a hundred deadly car accidents. So falling from&nbsp;
around 15 feet onto an airplane runway probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.080
doesn't do a lot of damage in this universe, even&nbsp;
moving at the speed of a plane about to take off.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.640
What is less believable in the Fast universe is&nbsp;
that she wouldn't immediately reconnect with Han,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.420
which is likely why they'll explain her&nbsp;
faked death the same way they explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.780
his -- through the intervention&nbsp;
of Mr. Nobody and The Agency.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.400
Aside from the fact that Nobody faked Han's&nbsp;
death for his own gain and could conceivably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.420
have done so for Gisele, his involvement&nbsp;
is really the only way the Antarctica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.740
prison-break subplot makes any sense. Brie&nbsp;
Larson's Tess is Mr. Nobody's daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.960
and her goal seems to be to help Letty escape, so&nbsp;
she would logically need to have a destination in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.240
mind for her. So Gisele's presence at the climax&nbsp;
of this storyline almost outright confirms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.560
she's been involved with The Agency since her&nbsp;
"death" and is there at the behest of Tess.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.060
Though we aren't yet sure what Tess, Gisele,&nbsp;
and The Agency are planning, it seems certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:25.920
that they've had this contingency ready for some&nbsp;
time. With Mr. Nobody and his ward, Little Nobody,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.600
out of commission, the treacherous Agent Aimes&nbsp;
has been given the proverbial keys to the kingdom.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.860
Perhaps Mr. Nobody noticed a change in&nbsp;
the tides and began to fear Dante and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:40.380
Aimes' nefarious takeover of the Agency. With&nbsp;
a sense of paranoia rivaling that of Nick Fury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:44.340
Nobody might have closed ranks and kept&nbsp;
his circle of trust as small as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:48.300
which would explain why he couldn't have&nbsp;
revealed that Gisele was still alive even to Han.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:56.400
"That means I can trust you. And that&nbsp;
is very rare in this line of work."

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.720
In this scenario, Nobody may have&nbsp;
sequestered the nuclear submarine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.520
as a last resort base of operations for&nbsp;
Tess, Gisele, and possibly even Hobbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.840
if he is also part of this clandestine&nbsp;
world-saving scheme. We're sure to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.640
low-down on what Gisele and Hobbs have been up to&nbsp;
in the upcoming sequel, or sequels, to "Fast X."

